# Appendix G — Extending Your Game: Math, Physics, State Machines & AI

Chapter 13 built a whole game with the basics. This appendix is the shelf of
things you reach for when the basics aren't enough: real math and physics,
game-as-state-machine thinking, and — the big one — how to give your enemies a
brain, first the classic way that runs on the bare console, then by calling out
to a language model. Every runnable claim here is confirmed at the prompt.

## Real math and physics are already in the box

You don't hand-roll square roots or kinematics — the `math` and `phys` libraries
are wired in. Ask for three at once:

```motoi
(display (list (math/pow 2 10)
               (phys/kinetic-energy 2 5)
               (phys/kinematics 0 5 2 3)))
(newline)
```

```
(1024 25 (24 11))
```

Read the three answers. `math/pow` gave `2^10 = 1024`. `phys/kinetic-energy` took
a mass of `2` and speed of `5` and returned `25` (½·m·v² = ½·2·25). And
`phys/kinematics` took a start position `0`, velocity `5`, acceleration `2`, over
time `3`, and returned `(24 11)` — the object ends at position `24` moving at
speed `11`. This is the physics a platformer's jump arc or a shot's trajectory is
made of, and it is one verb away. `math/pi`, `math/sin`, `math/sqrt`, and the
rest are there too — the science of motion, ready to use.

## A game is a state machine — and you can make the type yourself

Appendix D showed one game *is* a state machine written in plain `cond`. Here is
the next step: a reusable state-machine **type** you build once and stamp out for
every enemy, boss, or door. It is a closure over a current state and a table of
rules — no library, just `lambda` and `let`:

```motoi
(define (make-machine start rules)
  (let ((state start))
    (lambda (event)
      (let scan ((rs rules))
        (cond ((null? rs) state)
              ((and (eq? (caar rs) state) (eq? (cadar rs) event))
               (set! state (caddar rs)) state)
              (else (scan (cdr rs))))))))

(define guard-ai
  (make-machine 'patrol
    (list (list 'patrol 'see-hero   'chase)
          (list 'chase  'reach-hero 'attack)
          (list 'chase  'lose-hero  'patrol)
          (list 'attack 'lose-hero  'patrol))))

(display (guard-ai 'see-hero))
(newline)
(display (guard-ai 'reach-hero))
(newline)
(display (guard-ai 'lose-hero))
(newline)
```

```
chase
attack
patrol
```

Read the guard's life. It starts on `patrol`; the event `see-hero` moved it to
`chase`; `reach-hero` moved it to `attack`; `lose-hero` sent it back to `patrol`.
Each `list` in the rules is *from-state, event, to-state* — the whole enemy is
that table. Make a second enemy with a different table and you have a different
personality from the same `make-machine`. This is the type of thinking that
scales a game up: a boss is more rows, a pause screen is one more state.

## Give an enemy a brain: behavior trees, running now

The `ai` library is a full kit of classic game AI that runs on the bare console —
no model, no network. The centrepiece is the **behavior tree**: you compose an
enemy's mind out of `ai/bt-sequence` (do all, in order), `ai/bt-selector` (try
until one works), `ai/bt-condition` (a test), and `ai/bt-action` (a deed), then
`ai/bt-tick` runs it. Wire a brain and tick it for a whole squad of enemies:

```motoi
(define log '())
(define (enemy-brain name)
  (ai/bt-sequence
    (ai/bt-condition (lambda () #t))
    (ai/bt-action (lambda () (set! log (cons name log))))))
(for-each (lambda (foe) (ai/bt-tick (enemy-brain foe)))
          '(goblin orc troll))
(display (reverse log))
(newline)
```

```
(goblin orc troll)
```

Each enemy got its own brain — a sequence that checks a condition, then acts —
and `ai/bt-tick` ran all three, one per foe, in order. The `log` proves each one
acted: `(goblin orc troll)`. That is how you control *many* enemies: build one
brain-maker, tick it per enemy every frame. The same library gives you steering
(`ai/seek`, `ai/flee`, `ai/pursue`) to move one enemy toward the hero, and
`ai/flock` to move a whole swarm with one call. None of it needs anything outside
the console.

## Give an enemy a brain: calling a language model

Now the other kind of AI — a **language model**. Motoi can call out to an LLM so
an enemy can *decide in words* or *taunt* the player. The verbs are `llm/ask`,
`ai/generate`, and `ai/classify`. Crucially, the model is **not** inside the
console: these verbs route to a language-model provider you wire up. If none is
configured, `llm/ask` answers `#f` instead of failing, so you write for both:

```motoi
(define (goblin-tactic)
  (let ((reply (llm/ask \"In one word, how should a cornered goblin act?\")))
    (if reply reply 'no-llm-configured)))
(display (goblin-tactic))
(newline)
```

```
no-llm-configured
```

At the bare prompt no model is wired, so `llm/ask` returned `#f` and the enemy
fell back to `no-llm-configured`. Wire a provider — a running language-model
service the console talks to — and the *same* call returns a real word like
`ambush` or `flee`, which your state machine above can act on. That is the whole
shape of LLM-in-a-game: ask the model in words, get words back, feed them to the
logic you already wrote. `ai/generate` does the same for longer text (a taunt, a
line of dialogue); `ai/classify` sorts a situation into a label.

> **Tip**
>
> Mix the two brains. Let the fast, local behavior tree run every frame for
> moment-to-moment action, and call the language model *rarely* — once when a
> fight starts, to pick a tactic or a taunt — then store its answer in the
> enemy's blackboard (`ai/bb-set!`) and let the state machine act on it. The
> model gives you variety; the local AI gives you speed.

> **Warning**
>
> The language-model verbs (`llm/ask`, `ai/generate`, `ai/classify`,
> `ai/summarize`) are **not** part of the bare console — they need an external
> model provider wired up, and without one they either return `#f` (`llm/ask`) or
> report `requires-…-sidecar` and stop. The *local* `ai` library — behavior
> trees, steering, flocking, pathfinding — needs nothing extra and always runs.
> Build your game so it plays without the model, and treat the model as flavour
> on top, not the floor under your feet.

